/*
  The interrogation. A revisit `<hub>` (§7.3.2): `pressLedger`/`pressLetter`
  press the two clues the crime scene logged. They carry no
  `when="holds(foundClue(...))"` guard because both clues are asserted on the
  only route here — the scene is sequenced `after:` the crime scene — so such
  a guard is always true; `check-project` reports it as `W-FACT-GUARANTEED`
  (dsl 0.20.0 §5). `askAlibi` is `once`; `leave` is the unguarded `exit` that
  satisfies the no-dead-end obligation. After the hub, a
  `<match on="run.suspectFocus">` reads the run-tier enum the choices wrote —
  exhaustive over the enum domain, so no `<otherwise>` is needed.
*/

## The Interview Room

@narrator: Three chairs, one table, and the smell of cold coffee.

<hub id="interrogate">
  <choice id="pressLedger" label="Press them on the ledger">
    @detective: These numbers were bled dry. Explain them.
    ::set{ run.suspectFocus = "blake" }
    ::set{ run.leadStrength += 1 }
  </choice>
  <choice id="pressLetter" label="Press them on the letter">
    @detective: You signed this, then you tried to unsign it.
    ::set{ run.suspectFocus = "blake" }
    ::set{ run.leadStrength += 1 }
  </choice>
  <choice id="askAlibi" label="Ask for an alibi" once>
    @detective: Walk me through last night. Slowly.
  </choice>
  <choice id="leave" label="End the interview" exit>
    @detective: We're done here. For now.
  </choice>
</hub>

## Reading the Room

<match on="run.suspectFocus">
  <when is="blake">
    @detective{mono}: Blake keeps surfacing. A thread worth pulling.
  </when>
  <when is="cass">
    @detective{mono}: Cass, then — the quiet ones keep the best books.
  </when>
  <when is="dana">
    @detective{mono}: Dana. I'm not convinced, but I'll note it.
  </when>
</match>
